<script>
// GET /play — the compiler, in the reader's browser.
//
// The front end is compiled to WebAssembly (`compiler/vyrn-play`) and answers
// three questions on this page: where the tokens are, what the diagnostics are,
// and what the program prints. All three come from the same Rust the `vyrn`
// binary is built from, so the page cannot disagree with the compiler.
//
// The pill says RUNNING and not BUILT, and it is the opposite of the guide's for
// the same reason the guide's is honest: a guide block ran once while the page
// was built, and this one runs when the reader presses the button. The caption
// says which engine — the tree-walking interpreter, which is the reference every
// other backend is checked against.
//
// EVERYTHING ON THIS PAGE IS MARKUP THE EXPORT WROTE. The examples are Vyrn data
// rendered into hidden `<pre>` blocks, escaped by the template, and `play.js`
// reads them back with `textContent`. Nothing here is a JavaScript string
// holding a program.
import { Head } from "std/ui"
import { pageHead } from "../nav"
import { playExamples, Example } from "../play"

export fn head() -> Head {
    return pageHead("Playground — Vyrn")
}

fn examples() -> Array<Example> {
    return playExamples()
}
</script>

<template>
<main class="page play" data-widget="play">
    <section class="hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">Playground</p>
        <h1 class="display">Type Vyrn. Run it here.</h1>
        <p class="lede">The compiler's front end is compiled to WebAssembly and runs in this tab. Nothing is uploaded, nothing is stored, and there is no server behind this page.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
        <div class="plate" data-play-plate>
            <div class="plate-head">
                <span class="pill">Running</span>
                <span class="eyebrow" data-play-status>Loading the compiler…</span>
                <span class="spacer"></span>
                <label class="pickbox">
                    <span class="sr-only">Example</span>
                    <select data-play-pick>
                        <option v-for="e in examples()" :key="e.id" :value="e.id">{{ e.title }}</option>
                    </select>
                </label>
                <button type="button" class="btn" data-play-share>Copy link</button>
                <button type="button" class="btn primary" data-play-run disabled="disabled">Run</button>
            </div>

            <div class="editor" data-play-editor>
                <pre class="code hl" aria-hidden="true"><code data-play-hl></code></pre>
                <textarea data-play-src spellcheck="false" autocapitalize="off" autocorrect="off" autocomplete="off" wrap="off" aria-label="Vyrn source"></textarea>
            </div>

            <p class="cap">Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> to run. <kbd>Tab</kbd> inserts two spaces. Colour comes from the compiler's own lexer, not from a second one written in JavaScript.</p>

            <details class="stdinbox" data-play-stdin-box>
                <summary>Standard input</summary>
                <textarea data-play-stdin spellcheck="false" aria-label="Standard input"></textarea>
                <p class="cap">These lines reach <code>readLine()</code>, one per call. At the end of them it answers <code>None</code>, exactly as a pipe does.</p>
            </details>

            <!-- Both panes are what RUNNING says, and running is a button press
                 with no page load behind it — so without a live region a screen
                 reader is told nothing at all about the result (RFC-0105 M4).
                 Polite, not assertive: the reader asked for this, and it can
                 wait for the sentence they are on. -->
            <div class="diags" data-play-diags hidden="hidden" aria-live="polite"></div>

            <div class="out" data-play-out aria-live="polite"><span class="dim">Nothing has run yet.</span></div>
        </div>

        <p class="notice" data-play-note>This is the <strong>tree-walking interpreter</strong> — the reference semantics, the same engine <code>vyrn run</code> uses, compiled to WebAssembly and running in this tab. The native and WebAssembly backends are checked against it byte for byte by the parity harness.</p>

        <dl class="nongoals">
            <dt>What runs</dt>
            <dd>One file, plus <code>std/</code>, which is embedded in the module. A relative import has nowhere to resolve to and says so.</dd>
            <dt>What refuses</dt>
            <dd><code>readFile</code>, <code>writeFile</code> and <code>listDir</code> answer with the same <code>Err</code> the compiler defines everywhere else — <code>cannot read `x`</code>, <code>cannot write `x`</code> — because a tab has no filesystem. <code>args()</code> is empty. The clock is read once, when the run starts.</dd>
            <dt>How deep it recurses</dt>
            <dd>The interpreter's limit is 1,000 nested calls, and it is the same number in every backend. A browser gives out before that: 466 to 468 here, measured by binary search against this page's own worker, because the engine puts every WebAssembly call on a native stack a worker gets half of. Past that the page says so, and says whose ceiling it was — <code>vyrn run</code> takes the same program to 1,000.</dd>
            <dt>How long it may run</dt>
            <dd>Five seconds. The program runs in a Web Worker, and terminating one is the only way to stop running WebAssembly — so a program that loops forever is stopped rather than asked to stop.</dd>
        </dl>
    </section>

    <div hidden="hidden" data-play-examples>
        <pre v-for="e in examples()" :key="e.id" :data-play-example="e.id">{{ e.src }}</pre>
        <pre v-for="e in examples()" :key="e.id" :data-play-example-stdin="e.id">{{ e.stdin }}</pre>
    </div>
</main>
</template>
